<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">combat</ansi>

How a battle works, from the first blow to the last. Each part has its own
help page, named in brackets.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The round</ansi>

Combat resolves once a combat round, every 8 seconds (two of the game's
4-second rounds). Every fighter on both sides takes their turn each combat
round: an attack, a round of a spell being cast, or waiting. A fight goes on
until one side is dead or gone, or nobody is fighting any more. What you
start mid-round, such as <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi> or a spell, waits for the next combat
round, up to 8 seconds. Spell rounds, waits, and combat statuses (bleeding,
staggered, and the rest) count combat rounds; other buffs and effects that
last a number of rounds count the game's 4-second rounds.

The round is decided at once, but its lines are paced out to you over a
few seconds, so you watch the fight unfold. Choose how fast with
<ansi fg="command">set combatpace fast</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">normal</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">slow</ansi>, or <ansi fg="command">off</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help combatpace</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your company fights as one (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">, </ansi><ansi fg="command">help targeting</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">, </ansi><ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

Enemies come in named groups ("A band of ruffians (4)"), and you fight a
whole group, never one member: <ansi fg="command">attack ruffians</ansi> starts the battle. From
then on it plays out on its own, from how your company was set up: only
<ansi fg="command">flee</ansi> takes you out, and the one order you can still give is a new
focus for the whole company (<ansi fg="command">company tactics focus [rule]</ansi>, once a round,
<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>). Set your formation, everyone's strategy (<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>),
and your company tactics first, and <ansi fg="command">scout</ansi> the group to see how it stands. Your companions fight beside you.
Once the battle starts:

- the whole enemy group fights, and each of you starts on the foe your
  strategy picks (the weakest you can reach, unless you set another rule
  or a company focus), and each enemy on the one its kind prefers;
- healers heal the hurt and casters cast their spells, with real mana;
- guardians step in front of their wards to take blows meant for them
  (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>);
- a blow that draws blood may break its target's chant (a heavy one
  always does), on either side, and
  a blow that would hit meets one defense: a shield blocks it, a weapon
  parries it, or the target dodges (<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>; a burdened
  fighter dodges less, <ansi fg="command">help burden</ansi>), and a
  shield-bearer may slam a blocked blow back (<ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>);
- a big foe, such as the forest ogre, may wind up a heavy blow in plain
  view, and only a critical hit that gets through the armor, or a
  stagger, knockdown, or stun, breaks it (<ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>);
- anyone left without a target, or with one they can't reach, turns to
  another by their strategy, and you're told about it
  (<ansi fg="command">You turn toward the bandit cutthroat.</ansi>);
- this goes on until the fight is over, or until you <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi>.

Where several groups are in one place, you fight them one at a time. The
others wait their turn without striking, and the next battle begins as
soon as one ends. Another player can take on a waiting group meanwhile.

In the web client, the Combat tab shows your battle as it goes: both
formations, who strikes whom, how hurt each enemy looks, the fallen, and
the groups waiting their turn (<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Who can strike whom (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

Both sides stand in a 3x3 formation: three columns, and three rows from the
front to the back. Where a fighter stands, and the reach of their weapon,
decide who they can strike. A fighter in the front row shields the one
behind them. <ansi fg="command">formation reach [member]</ansi> shows who a member can reach, and
<ansi fg="command">scout [group]</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help scout</ansi>) shows an enemy group's formation and which
of them you can reach.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Hitting (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help attack</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">, </ansi><ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

Your chance to hit comes from your speed against your target's. Several
things add to it or take it away:

- <ansi fg="yellow">Chemistry</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>): a company that has served together
  a long time gets up to +6% to hit.
- <ansi fg="yellow">Darkness</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help light</ansi>): fighting in the dark costs -40% to hit, and in
  dim light -10%.
- <ansi fg="yellow">Dual wielding</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help dual-wield</ansi>): an off-hand weapon swings with a
  penalty.

A blow that would hit then meets the target's block, parry, or dodge, and
what gets through is cut by their armor (<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help armor</ansi>).

A sharpened blade (<ansi fg="command">help sharpen</ansi>) does +1 damage on each strike while its
edge lasts.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Falling (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help death</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">, </ansi><ansi fg="command">help resurrect</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

At 0 health you drop. At -10 you die: you lose a level and wake at the last
city's church, with your living company. A companion who dies stays on your
roster for a while, and can be raised at a church or a village shaman.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Reading a fight (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

Every blow gets a line, and its numbers sit in brackets at the end:
<ansi fg="command">(5 damage)</ansi>, or <ansi fg="command">(critical hit, 9 damage, bleeding)</ansi> for a critical hit, which
also names the status it leaves. A line with no brackets is a miss, a
block, a parry, or a dodge. A damaging critical hit that leaves someone
standing gets a pain reaction right after the blow; a fatal one gets a
death line instead. The reactions are only text (<ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi>). One
line opens a fight and one closes it.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Statuses (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

A critical hit leaves a mark that matches the weapon: swords and claws make
you bleed, maces stagger, hammers and axes knock down or break armor, bows
leave you exposed. Staggered and knocked-down fighters lose an action,
stunned ones two, and can't block, parry, or dodge meanwhile (<ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>).
Statuses last a few combat rounds and end with the fight.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Wounds (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

A critical hit on you or a companion also leaves a wound, which stops
healing short of your maximum until it is treated. After the fight,
<ansi fg="command">heal wounds</ansi> has your clerics, bandages, and splints tend the company;
an inn stay knits what is left, and a camp rest one wound per splint or
bandage.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">After the fight (</ansi><ansi fg="command">help battle-summary</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold">)</ansi>

When your company's fight ends, you see a summary. It shows the damage each
side did, who did the most, the highest hit, each side's blocks, parries,
and dodges, kills, how each enemy ended,
and your company's health.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Commands</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">attack [group]</ansi>    start a fight with a group
  <ansi fg="command">scout [group]</ansi>     see how a group stands
  <ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi>          how each of you fights: roles and targets
  <ansi fg="command">company tactics</ansi>   the company's focus and healing threshold
  <ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>     guardians and their guards
  <ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>      blocks, parries, and dodges
  <ansi fg="command">help burden</ansi>       how your own load cuts your dodge
  <ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>   broken chants, wind-ups, and shield counters
  <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi>              try to run out of the room (the only way out of a battle)
  <ansi fg="command">consider [target]</ansi> size up a foe
  <ansi fg="command">formation</ansi>         see and arrange your company's formation
  <ansi fg="command">company chemistry</ansi> your company's chemistry
  <ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>     what a critical hit leaves behind
  <ansi fg="command">heal wounds</ansi>       tend the company's wounds after a fight
  <ansi fg="command">sharpen</ansi>           sharpen your company's blades
  <ansi fg="command">light</ansi>             how well you can see here
  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace</ansi>    how fast a round's lines come to you

In the tutorial, the Practice Yard lets you try all of this against straw
soldiers that can't hurt you (<ansi fg="command">help tutorial</ansi>).
Repeated enemy names become fixed labels such as the first cutthroat and
the second cutthroat. A survivor keeps its label when another falls; see
<ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi> for pronouns and names.

Some enemies surrender or flee when their group breaks (help morale). After victory, answer each surrendered foe’s mercy question (help mercy).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Member orders</ansi>

Members fight automatically. Safe <ansi fg="command">ask [member]</ansi> orders
manage gear and provisions outside battles; conversation and observation
remain available in battle. Old queued orders recheck ownership and presence.
See <ansi fg="command">help ask</ansi> for the compatibility commands.
